# Authentication and authorization

## Auth state

- Centralize session in a Pinia auth store: access token, user identity, role, permission list, login/logout.
- Persist the access token in **`sessionStorage`** for this learning project (survives refresh; cleared when the browser session ends). Revisit only if the app later needs a different policy.
- Centralize all token read, write, and remove behind the auth store (or a tiny token helper used only by that store / the Axios request interceptor). Do not call `sessionStorage` / `localStorage` from components or feature services.
- Never store passwords, JWT signing keys, or other secrets in frontend source or env files committed to git.
- Do not hardcode login credentials in frontend code. Seeded local accounts are documented in the repository README and project docs — not in Cursor rules.

## Router

- Protect private routes with navigation guards (`requiresAuth`, optional `permissions`).
- Redirect unauthenticated users to login; send authenticated users away from the login page when appropriate.
- Permission checks on routes and in the UI control **visibility and UX only**.

## Permissions

- The backend JWT includes repeated claims named `permission` (claim type `"permission"`), one value per granted right (for example `User_View`, `Role_Edit`).
- The frontend may decode the JWT payload after login to collect every `permission` claim into a list for UI visibility and route guards. Support multiple values for the same claim name.
- Prefer those permission names, not hardcoded business role names (`Admin`, `User`).
- Decoded JWT data is **not** verified authorization. Hide or disable UI the user cannot use, but assume the backend still enforces policies. Always expect 403/404 from the API.

## Token expiry / 401

- On HTTP **401** from the shared Axios client: the response interceptor delegates to the auth/session handler. That handler clears auth state (including the stored token), stops further authenticated calls, and navigates to login (preserve return URL if useful).
- Do not log the user out solely because a response body has `success: false`.
- JWT permissions only seed the UI. The store refreshes from `GET /api/auth/me` when `X-Access-Version` changes, on 403, before permission routes, on tab focus, and every 60 s. Never require a re-login for rights changes.
